// expect-error: R003
//
// R003 — inner-ring code cannot call extern functions.
//
// Before step 24 of the supremum loop (axis 6, "fewer escape hatches")
// this exact source compiled cleanly and produced WASM with a direct
// FFI call from inner-ring code. The ring_check pass had a no-op for
// inner-ring modules with the comment "forward-looking — no extern
// syntax exists yet, so this is a no-op." Extern syntax DOES exist
// today, so the no-op was a real bypass.
//
// ABUSE VECTOR (verified against the pre-change commit):
// An inner-ring module declares `extern "C" fn foo() ! { FFI, Unsafe }`
// and calls it from its own function. The compiler accepted this and
// emitted a WASM module with an FFI call running in the inner ring —
// exactly the privilege the ring system is meant to quarantine to the
// outer ring.
//
// After step 24, ring_check's Inner-ring branch walks each function
// body for `TypedExprKind::ExternCall` and emits R003 with the
// offending callee name. The diagnostic suggests two copyable fixes
// (move to outer/trusted, or use `grant(&cap, fn(ref) -> ...)` to
// cross the ring).
//
// NOTE (step 30 / E003 update): use_it must NOT declare
// `! { FFI, Unsafe }` in its effect row, because step 30's E003
// check rejects inner-ring functions that declare privilege
// effects (Unsafe / FFI). The R003 check (body-level extern call)
// still fires on the call to foo() regardless of caller's effect
// row — R003 is what this fixture pins.
module ext;

extern "C" fn foo() -> i64 ! { FFI, Unsafe };

fn use_it() -> i64 @Internal {
    return foo();
}
